{
  "dom_id": "step-e9090",
  "name": "Example: Identifying an Orthogonal Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix",
  "problem": "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ for every matrix $A?$\n\n- $\\text{Null}(A)$\n\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $\\text{Null}(A^T)$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ is the row space of $A,$ and consists of all linear combinations of the rows of $A.$\n\nIf a vector is orthogonal to every linear combination of the rows of $A,$ it must be orthogonal to each row of $A.$\nBut notice that when pre-multiplying a vector by $A,$ we multiply each row of $A$ by that vector.\nSo, $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ must be orthogonal to the null space of $A,$ which is $\\text{Null}(A).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14093810/assets/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
